I like it but only one hint.
Instead of set video configuration in constructor you can get it from MediaFormat like this:
```java
    @Override
    public void setVideoFormat(MediaFormat videoFormat, boolean isOnlyVideo) {
        int width = videoFormat.getInteger(MediaFormat.KEY_WIDTH);
        int height = videoFormat.getInteger(MediaFormat.KEY_HEIGHT);
        int fps = videoFormat.getInteger(MediaFormat.KEY_FRAME_RATE);
        int videoBitrate = videoFormat.getInteger(MediaFormat.KEY_BIT_RATE);
    }

    @Override
    public void setAudioFormat(MediaFormat audioFormat, boolean isOnlyVideo) {
        int sampleRate = audioFormat.getInteger(MediaFormat.KEY_SAMPLE_RATE);
        int channels = audioFormat.getInteger(MediaFormat.KEY_CHANNEL_COUNT);
        int audioBitrate = audioFormat.getInteger(MediaFormat.KEY_BIT_RATE);
        //I should get a way to notify you this if necessary. It isn't a proper way to do it.
        int sampleSize = new MicrophoneManager(null).getMaxInputSize();
    }
```

This is the real usage of this methods and by this way you can make it really easy to use because you don't need know anything about your configuration. It is configured with stream object parameters.

You will get this info after start is called so the idea is wait until you have all configuration to start record. For that reason I have STARTED state.
You will need set state STARTED waiting for configuration and RECORDING after get configuration and start the real record.
